            "text": "The first light of morning enters quietly, not as an announcement but as a suggestion. It slides across the floor, touches the leg of a chair, and waits.\n\nOutside, a delivery truck grumbles awake. Somewhere, a dog barks once, then seems to reconsider the effort. I make coffee and watch steam lift from the mug like a small, temporary cloud.\n\nThere is something comforting about beginnings that do not demand anything. The day has not yet become a list of obligations. It is only a stretch of possibility, open and unclaimed.\n\nEven the unfinished things on my desk look gentler in this light. Papers lean against notebooks; a pen rests uncapped, as if interrupted mid-thought. I could begin anywhere: answer the message, wash the dishes, learn a song, or simply sit long enough to hear the house settling around me.\n\nSilence is not empty. It contains refrigerator hum, distant traffic, the soft click of pipes, and my own breathing, steady as a tide.\n\nPerhaps this is why people keep returning to mornings: they offer a brief truce between what happened yesterday and what might happen next. In that narrow interval, we are allowed to be unfinished without being failures. We can change our minds, forgive ourselves, and choose a different direction before anyone notices we have moved.\n\nThe coffee cools. The light grows stronger. Soon, the phone will ring, and the world will ask for answers. For now, though, I lift the mug, take one slow sip, and begin."
          }
